.TH ATOF 3
.SH NAME
atof, atoi, atol, strtod, strtol, strtoul \- convert ASCII to numbers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <stdlib.h>

double
atof(nptr)
char *nptr;

int
atoi(nptr)
char *nptr;

long
atol(nptr)
char *nptr;

double
strtod(nptr, endptr)
char *nptr, **endptr;

long
strtol(nptr, endptr, base)
char *nptr, **endptr;
int base;

unsigned long
strtoul(nptr, endptr, base)
char *nptr, **endptr;
int base;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
These functions convert the number written at the beginning of the
string
.I nptr
into a value of the type each returns. Each skips white space at the
start of the string, then reads an optional sign and the digits of the
number, and stops at the first character that cannot be part of it.
.PP
.B strtod
reads a floating point number: digits, with an optional decimal point,
then optionally e or E and an exponent, a signed integer. Its result is
the double nearest the number's value, correctly rounded however many
digits are written.
.B atof(nptr)
is
.BR "strtod(nptr, (char **)0)" .
.PP
.B strtol
and
.B strtoul
read an integer in the base
.IR base ,
from 2 to 36, in which the letters a to z, or A to Z, are the digits
from 10 to 35. If
.I base
is 0, the number is read as C writes it: hexadecimal after a 0x or 0X,
octal after a 0, and decimal otherwise. With base 16, a 0x or 0X may
come before the digits.
.PP
When
.I endptr
is not NULL, strtod, strtol and strtoul store in *\fIendptr\fP a pointer
to the character after the number, or
.I nptr
itself if no number was found.
.PP
.B atoi
and
.B atol
read a decimal integer.
.SH "RETURN VALUES"
The value, or 0 if there was no number. When the value is too large for
a double, strtod returns HUGE_VAL, an infinity, with the number's sign;
when it is too small to be told from 0, it returns 0.
.SH ERRORS
strtod sets errno to ERANGE when the value is too large or too small.
.SH SEE ALSO
scanf(3), ecvt(3), printf(3), ctype(3)
.SH HISTORY
The
.B atof
and
.B atoi
functions first appeared in Version 1 AT&T UNIX. Version 7 has
.BR atol .
.B strtod
and
.B strtol
are those of System V.
.PP
.B strtoul
is not in the C libraries of Version 7 AT&T UNIX, System V or 4.3BSD.
.SH BUGS
strtol, strtoul, atoi and atol do not detect overflow: a number too
large for the type wraps around.
